Use this as a workaround when your Windows ACME client fails to issue a certificate.

1. Request the certificate

On Your Linux or WSL:

sudo certbot certonly --manual --preferred-challenges http -d your-domain.com

2. Create the challenge folder

On the Windows Server, open PowerShell as Administrator:

```powershell
$webRoot = "C:\path\to\your\website"

$challengePath = Join-Path $webRoot ".well-known\acme-challenge"

New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $challengePath -Force | Out-Null
```

3. Allow IIS to serve extensionless files

Run:

```powershell
$webConfigPath = Join-Path $challengePath "web.config"

@'
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<configuration>
  <system.webServer>
    <staticContent>
      <remove fileExtension="." />
      <mimeMap fileExtension="." mimeType="text/plain" />
    </staticContent>

    <handlers>
      <clear />
      <add name="ACME-StaticFile"
           path="*"
           verb="GET,HEAD"
           modules="StaticFileModule"
           resourceType="File"
           requireAccess="Read" />
    </handlers>
  </system.webServer>
</configuration>
'@ | Set-Content `
  -Path $webConfigPath `
  -Encoding UTF8
```

Restart IIS if the configuration is not applied immediately:

```powershell
iisreset
```

4. Create the challenge file

Replace the placeholders with the values shown by Certbot:

```powershell
$token = "<TOKEN_FROM_CERTBOT>"
$content = "<CONTENT_FROM_CERTBOT>"

Set-Content `
    -Path (Join-Path $challengePath $token) `
    -Value $content `
    -NoNewline
```

5. Verify the challenge

```powershell
curl.exe "http://your-domain.com/.well-known/acme-challenge/$token"
```

The response must exactly match `$content`.

Only after this works, return to Certbot and press Enter.

6. Convert the certificate to PFX

```bash
sudo openssl pkcs12 -export \
  -out certificate.pfx \
  -inkey /etc/letsencrypt/live/your-domain.com/privkey.pem \
  -in /etc/letsencrypt/live/your-domain.com/fullchain.pem
```
  
7. Copy the PFX to the Windows Server

The PFX contains the private key. Do not upload it to GitHub or share it publicly.

8. Import the certificate

```powershell
$password = Read-Host "Enter PFX password" -AsSecureString

Import-PfxCertificate `
    -FilePath "C:\Temp\certificate.pfx" `
    -CertStoreLocation Cert:\LocalMachine\My `
    -Password $password
```

9. Bind the certificate in IIS

Open:

IIS Manager
→ Sites
→ Your Site
→ Bindings
→ HTTPS
→ Edit

Select the imported certificate and click OK.

```powershell
iisreset
```
10. Clean up

After confirming HTTPS works, delete the PFX file:

```powershell
Remove-Item "C:\Temp\certificate.pfx" -Force
```

Keep the challenge-folder `web.config` for future renewals.

Certificates issued with manual Certbot validation do not renew automatically. Repeat this process before the certificate expires.
